# lucent — build prompt
<!--⁠‌‌​‌​‌​‌‌‌​‌​‌‌​‌​‌‌‌​‌​​​​‌​‌‌‌​​​‌​​‌‌​​​‌​‌‌‌⁠ uberdesigner prompt format v1 · lucent · v1.0.0 -->

## Goal
You're building a two-screen, scroll-driven website for a fictional contact lens brand called "Lucent". It opens on a
full-screen portrait of a woman that sharpens out of a heavy blur while the navigation, headline (letter by letter) and
copy rise into place. Scrolling then plays one pinned scene: the camera zooms into her eye, a glowing cyan scanner
draws itself over the iris, the hero copy scrolls away as the features copy scrolls in, the eye washes out to the page
colour, a single lens settles in and then splits sideways into its four layers. Hovering one of the four feature cards
turns it blue and lights a glowing dot on its layer. All of the motion is built in code from still images, with no
video: the zoom redraws one 4K portrait on a canvas, the scanner is an SVG, and the lens is four transparent images.
Match everything below exactly: layout, type, colours, images and motion. Keep the copy and images as given; the user
swaps in their own content afterwards.

## Stack and setup
- Vite + React 19 + TypeScript. Plain CSS: one global tokens file plus CSS Modules per component. No CSS framework.
- Libraries: `gsap` 3.15 with its `ScrollTrigger` plugin (`import { ScrollTrigger } from "gsap/ScrollTrigger"`,
  `gsap.registerPlugin(ScrollTrigger)`). Nothing else.
- Create and run:
  ```bash
  npm create vite@latest lucent -- --template react-ts --no-interactive
  cd lucent
  npm install gsap
  npm run dev
  ```
- Folder layout:
  ```
  index.html                          font, title, empty favicon, portrait preload
  src/main.tsx                        renders <App /> and <Cursor />
  src/App.tsx                         page structure, intro timeline, scroll timeline (+ App.module.css)
  src/styles/tokens.css               all design tokens as CSS variables
  src/styles/global.css               reset and base styles
  src/components/Header.tsx           fixed header (+ Header.module.css)
  src/components/Hero.tsx             first screen's copy, callout card, buttons (+ Hero.module.css)
  src/components/Chars.tsx            a headline line split into one span per character (+ Chars.module.css)
  src/components/Features.tsx         second screen's copy and the four cards (+ Features.module.css)
  src/components/PortraitCanvas.tsx   the portrait canvas and the zoom (+ PortraitCanvas.module.css)
  src/components/Hud.tsx              the eye scanner, one SVG (+ Hud.module.css)
  src/components/LensStack.tsx        the four lens layers and their glow dots (+ LensStack.module.css)
  src/components/Cursor.tsx           custom cursor (+ Cursor.module.css)
  ```
- Delete the scaffold's leftovers that aren't in this layout (`App.css`, `index.css`, `src/assets/`, the sample icons
  in `public/`). Make sure TypeScript runs with `"strict": true` (add it to the scaffold's tsconfig if it isn't there).
- `global.css` sets `box-sizing: border-box` on everything (`*, *::before, *::after`), with margins and paddings at 0:
  every size below includes padding and borders.
- Page title: "Lucent — See everything. Feel nothing."; meta description: "A new class of daily silicone hydrogel lenses
  engineered for exceptional oxygen flow, moisture retention, and zero-gravity comfort all day long."
- `index.html` gets `<link rel="icon" href="data:,">` (no favicon 404) and
  `<link rel="preload" as="fetch" href="<portrait URL>" crossorigin>`: the portrait is loaded with `fetch()`, so an
  `as="image"` preload wouldn't be reused.
- Keep `<StrictMode>` in `main.tsx`. Every effect cleans up after itself (ignore a stale load, revert GSAP with
  `gsap.context().revert()`, which also kills the ScrollTrigger, cancel animation frames, disconnect observers),
  because StrictMode runs effects twice in development. Don't return a function from the `gsap.context()` callback:
  GSAP calls it on revert.
- State: `App` owns the timelines. It loads the portrait into an `ImageBitmap` and passes it to `PortraitCanvas`
  together with a mutable ref `{ zoom: 0, extra: 0 }` that the scroll timeline tweens and the canvas reads every
  frame. `App` also owns `active: number | null` (the active feature card) and passes it to `Features` and
  `LensStack`.
- `App` reaches the elements it animates through data attributes and `gsap.utils.selector(rootRef.current)`, since
  CSS Modules hash the class names: `data-reveal` = `header`, `label`, `headline`, `body`, `buttons`, `scroll`,
  `callout`; `data-scene` = `scene`, `portrait` (the canvas), `wash`, `hud` (the svg), `hud-ring`, `hud-scan`,
  `hud-mesh`, `lens`, `track`; `data-line` on each headline line, `data-char` on each character, `data-draw` on each
  scanner stroke.
- Visual values live in `tokens.css`: colours, type, the shared spacing and radii below. Positions and sizes that
  belong to one block go in its CSS Module as `calc(N * var(--u))`. Motion numbers (times, eases, the iris position)
  live as named constants at the top of the component that uses them.
- Never centre an element that GSAP animates with its own CSS `translate`: GSAP 3 folds that `translate` into its own
  pixel transform when it first animates the element, and the centring then goes stale when the window resizes. The
  scanner is centred by a wrapper `div` and GSAP animates the `svg` inside it. Elements GSAP doesn't touch (the lens
  layers, the glow dots) can use `translate`.
- Give every `<img>` its file's `width` and `height` attributes (from the asset table), so its box has the right
  shape before the file arrives.

## Design tokens
**Scale.** All sizes below are design pixels. Desktop (width ≥ 1024 px) is designed at 1440 × 900:
1 design px = `min(100vw / 1440, 100svh / 760)`. Phones and tablets (width < 1024 px) use a layout designed 415 design
px across: 1 design px = `min(100vw / 415, 100svh / 760)` (so on a short phone the layout is a little wider than 415
design px, and on a tablet much wider; the edges and anchors below keep it in place). So the design scales with the viewport width, and shrinks to fit on
screens shorter than 760 design px. Put this in a `--u` variable on `:root`, swap it in a `max-width: 1023px` media
query, and write every size as `calc(N * var(--u))`. The canvas needs the same unit in JS: measure a hidden probe
`div` that is `calc(100 * var(--u))` wide and divide by 100. Borders (1 px) and the blur radii in Motion are CSS
pixels, not design pixels.

