## Motion
### M1 · Intro
t = 0 is the moment the portrait is loaded and decoded (`fetch` → `blob()` → `createImageBitmap`) and both font
faces have loaded: wait for `document.fonts.load('400 1em "Instrument Sans"')` and
`document.fonts.load('500 1em "Instrument Sans"')`, not `document.fonts.ready`, which can resolve before the fonts
have started loading. Until then the page is just the page colour (the portrait is about 340 KB). If the portrait
fails, log the error and still play everything without it.
Everything the intro reveals starts at opacity 0 in CSS, so write each row as a `fromTo` with explicit end values
(`opacity: 1, y: 0, filter: "blur(0px)"`); a `from` would tween 0 to 0. Times in seconds, all `power2.out`:
| At | What | From → to | Duration |
|---|---|---|---|
| 0 | portrait canvas | opacity 0, `blur(40px)` → 1, `blur(0)` | 0.6 |
| 0.1 | the whole header (its divider rises with it) | opacity 0, y +24, `blur(8px)` → 1, 0, 0 | 0.45 |
| 0.1 | eyebrow | opacity 0, y +20, `blur(8px)` → normal | 0.45 |
| 0.35 | bottom paragraph | opacity 0, y +30, `blur(8px)` → normal | 0.5 |
| 0.35 | headline line 1, per character | opacity 0, `yPercent: 35`, `blur(12px)` → normal, stagger 0.02 | 0.4 each |
| 0.47 | headline line 2, per character | same, its own stagger of 0.02 | 0.4 each |
| 0.55 | button row | opacity 0, y +20, `blur(8px)` → normal | 0.5 |
| 0.6 | scroll hint | same | 0.5 |
| 0.95 | callout (card + pager) | opacity 0, y +16, `blur(10px)` → normal | 0.4 |
- Set the `<h1>` to opacity 1 at 0.35 (its characters carry the fade).
- Every intro tween ends with `clearProps: "filter,transform"` (the canvas: `"filter"`), so no element keeps a
  `filter` or a transform around.
- It plays once and isn't interrupted. Scrolling during it is fine: the scroll timeline touches other properties.

### M2 · Scroll scene
One GSAP timeline, `defaults: { ease: "none" }`, driven by ScrollTrigger on `.scene` with `start: "top top"`,
`end: "bottom bottom"`, `scrub: 1` (it catches up with the scroll over 1 s, which gives the smooth glide). The
timeline is 3 units long (end it with `tl.set({}, {}, 3)`), so each unit is one screen of scrolling.
| Units | What | From → to | Ease |
|---|---|---|---|
| 0 → 1 | portrait `zoom` (M3) | 0 → 1 | `power1.inOut` |
| 0.65 → 1.05 | scanner svg | opacity 0, scale 0.9 → 1, 1 | `power2.out` |
| 0.65 → 1.05 | every scanner stroke | `strokeDashoffset` 1 → 0 | `power2.out` |
| 0.65 → 1.7 | the ring's group | `rotation` −20 → 25, `svgOrigin: "0 0"` | none |
| 0.75 → 1.05 | scan lines group | opacity 0 → 1 | none |
| 0.95 → 1.2 | mesh group | opacity 0, scale 0.92 → 1, 1 (`svgOrigin: "0 0"`) | `power2.out` |
| 1.0 → 1.7 | track | `yPercent` 0 → −50 (up one screen) | `power1.inOut` |
| 1.4 → 1.75 | portrait `extra` (M3) | 0 → 1 | `power1.in` |
| 1.4 → 1.75 | wash | opacity 0 → 1 | `power1.inOut` |
| 1.4 → 1.7 | scanner svg | opacity → 0, scale → 1.3 | `power1.in` |
| 1.5 → 1.9 | lens box | opacity 0 → 1, scale 1.6 → 1, blur 16 px → 0 | `power2.out` |
| 2.2 → 2.8 | lens box | `--spread` 0 → 1 (GSAP tweens custom properties) | `power2.inOut` |
| 2.8 → 3.0 | hold | | |
- When a later row animates an element that an earlier row started (the scanner svg fading out at 1.4), use `.to()`:
  a second `fromTo` would render its start values immediately and overwrite the first.
- The lens blur: tween a plain object `{ px: 16 → 0 }` and in `onUpdate` set the box's `style.filter` to
  `blur(<px>px)` only while px is between 0.05 and 15.95, and to `""` otherwise, so the lens never idles in a filter
  layer (before it appears or once it's sharp). These writes are outside GSAP, so clear `style.filter` in the effect's
  cleanup after `ctx.revert()`.
- So at the top of the page the hero is still, the zoom plays with the hero copy in place, the copy scrolls out while
  the scanner is up, and the features copy arrives as the eye washes out.

### M3 · Portrait zoom (the hard bit)
The canvas redraws the visible part of the 4K source every frame, so the eye stays as sharp as the image allows; a
CSS transform would scale a cached bitmap. The iris to zoom into is at (2248, 907) in the image's own pixels, radius
36. At `zoom = 1` the iris is at the centre of the screen with radius 210 design px (phone: 150). The scale grows
exponentially with `zoom` (an even zoom speed), and the iris travels to the centre in step with the scale, which is
the same as zooming about one fixed point. `extra` then pushes in a further 30% about the centre.
```ts
const IRIS = { x: 2248, y: 907, r: 36 };       // source px
const IRIS_ON_SCREEN = mobile ? 150 : 210;     // design px, mobile = (max-width: 1023px)

// every frame (W, H = canvas CSS size, unit = probe width / 100); skip if W or H is 0, or nothing changed
const s0 = Math.max(W / img.width, H / img.height);
const left0 = (W - img.width * s0) / 2;
const top0 = (H - img.height * s0) * 0.25;
const q0 = { x: left0 + IRIS.x * s0, y: top0 + IRIS.y * s0 };   // the iris on screen at zoom 0
const s1 = (IRIS_ON_SCREEN * unit) / IRIS.r;
const s = s0 * Math.pow(s1 / s0, zoom);
const w = (s - s0) / (s1 - s0);
const qx = q0.x + (W / 2 - q0.x) * w;
const qy = q0.y + (H / 2 - q0.y) * w;
const scale = s * (1 + 0.3 * extra);
ctx.setTransform(1, 0, 0, 1, 0, 0);
ctx.imageSmoothingEnabled = true;
ctx.imageSmoothingQuality = "high";
ctx.clearRect(0, 0, canvas.width, canvas.height);
// only the visible part of the source, into the whole canvas
ctx.drawImage(img, IRIS.x - qx / scale, IRIS.y - qy / scale, W / scale, H / scale, 0, 0, canvas.width, canvas.height);
```
Run it in a `requestAnimationFrame` loop that redraws only when `zoom`, `extra` or the size changed. Resize the
backing store from a `ResizeObserver` on the canvas.

### M4 · Scanner
Covered by M2: it scales in from 0.9 while its strokes draw from their start points (arcs clockwise, the line from the
centre outwards, the circles from the top and the bottom), the scan lines fade in, the ring turns 45° over its life,
the mesh fades and grows in last, and the whole scanner swells to 1.3 as it fades with the wash. Its blend mode
(`screen`) makes the bloom add light to the eye.

### M5 · Lens
Covered by M2: the four stacked layers come in as one lens through the wash, from 1.6× and blurred, then fan out to
their four positions.

### M6 · Active card
Described in Layout → 5 and 6: the card turns blue and grows its arrow row, and its layer's glow dot fades in,
both 300 ms with the hover curve. It works at any scroll position.

### M7 · Cursor follow
The disc eases toward the pointer, covering 30% of the remaining distance per frame at 60 fps (frame-rate
independent: `k = 1 - 0.7^(dt / 16.67ms)`).

### Reduced motion (`prefers-reduced-motion: reduce`)
No zoom, no scanner, no blur, no stagger, and the lens doesn't split. The headline keeps its per-character spans
(they just don't animate). The intro fades the canvas and everything else in together over 0.4 s, `power1.out`. The scroll timeline keeps the track's scroll (1.0 → 1.7), fades the wash in over 1.4 → 1.75 and
the lens box over 1.5 → 1.9, with `--spread` already at 1 so the layers appear in their final places. The cursor and
the active cards stay.

